      "claim": "Caffeine does not directly suppress the secretion of growth hormone or IGF-1 in humans.",
      "evidence": "Systematic reviews of pediatric caffeine exposure report no statistically significant changes in circulating GH or IGF-1 after moderate caffeine doses.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "While caffeine can affect sleep architecture, moderate consumption does not cause the chronic, severe sleep deprivation required to significantly alter GH pulses or final adult height.",
      "evidence": "Longitudinal studies (e.g., Miller et al., 2022) show that daily caffeine ≤200 mg results in negligible sleep reduction and no effect on growth velocity or final height.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Large observational cohorts find no correlation between habitual moderate coffee consumption and final adult height.",
      "evidence": "Analyses of NHANES and European adolescent nutrition studies controlling for genetics and diet show comparable mean adult heights between caffeine consumers and non-consumers.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1, 3]
    }
